<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maues</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 85–60 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Moga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maues was the first major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, ruling in the early 1st century BCE. He established Saka power in Gandhara and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a>, issuing coins that copied Greek types. The Taxila <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plate inscription of Patika names him as the great king Moga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maues.jpg" alt="Maues" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maues_on_horse_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maues's name is known mainly from his coins, in the Gandhari form Moa and the Greek form Maúēs, both variants of the Saka name *Mava, meaning tiger and hero. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> copper plate gives it as Moga, from the Saka *Mauka with the same meaning, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> lion capital inscription calls him Mevaki Miyika. The Sakas were nomadic Eastern Iranian peoples, closely associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pahlavas">Pahlavas</a>. Some scholars, following Sten Konow's edition of the Corpus Inscriptionum Indicarum (1929), have suggested that Maues was of Kamboja lineage; others see him as an Iranian from the east, Parthian, Sistani Persian or Saka.</p><p>How and when Maues reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> is uncertain. The Han Shu records that when the Ta Yüeh-chih turned west and expelled the king of the Sai, the Sai moved south and crossed the Suspended Crossing. Inscriptions bearing Maues's name in the Chilas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> area suggest that this route, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> into the Gilgit valley, was used in his time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Maues, the first recorded ruler of the Sakas in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> region, took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a> of Kings, an enlarged form of a traditional Persian royal title. He seized key cities along the Indus from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> kingdoms, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and Pushkalavati, the capital of Gandhara; at Taxila he overstruck coins of Archebius and Apollodotus II. Saka power reached as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> during his reign. The Moga inscription is dated in the seventy-eighth year of the Great King, the Great Moga, and an inscription at Maira in the Salt Range may name him as Moasa.</p><p>Some of his coins were issued jointly with a queen, Machene, who may have been a daughter of one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> houses, and the Indo-Greek king Artemidoros called himself son of Maues on his own coins. A few square coins show a seated figure holding a sword, more probably the king himself than an early image of the Buddha. Others carry the lion, a Buddhist symbol since the Mauryan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, and the bull of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, which suggests wide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>; whether his support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was sincere or political is unclear.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maues” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Indo-Greeks were finally conquered by the Indo-Scythians: <b>Maues</b> ruled briefly in the northwest around 80 BCE, Azes I defeated Hippostratus in 48/47 BCE, and Rajuvula took the last kingdom around 10 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>There Indo-Scythian kings such as <b>Maues</b> and Azes ruled in the first century BCE, and Saka dynasties of satraps lasted for centuries: the Western Kshatrapas governed Gujarat and Malwa until about 400 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 85 BCE their king <b>Maues</b> established himself in Gandhara and took Taxila from the Indo-Greek Kingdom, founding a Saka kingdom in north-western India.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a></li></ul></section>
